</table>

<p class="lt-chem-154797">What is the concentration of Ca<sup>2</sup><sup>+</sup> in a water sample if its cell potential is found to be &ndash;0.084 V?</p>

<p class="lt-chem-154797"><strong>Solution</strong></p>

<p class="lt-chem-154797">Linear regression gives the calibration curve in Figure 11.2.16
, with an equation of</p>

<p class="lt-chem-154797">\[E_{\mathrm{cell}}=0.027+0.0303 \log \left[\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}\right] \nonumber\]</p>

<p class="lt-chem-154797">Substituting the sample&rsquo;s cell potential gives the concentration of Ca<sup>2</sup><sup>+</sup> as \(2.17 \times 10^{-4}\) M. Note that the slope of the calibration curve, which is 0.0303, is slightly larger than its ideal value of 0.05916/2 = 0.02958; this is not unusual and is one reason for using multiple standards.</p>
</section>

<section class="box-emphasis">
<p class="lt-chem-154797">One reason that it is not unusual to find that the experimental slope deviates from its ideal value of 0.05916/<em>n </em>is that this ideal value assumes that the temperature is 25&deg;C.</p>
</section>

